  # SharePoint list item retrieve-and-notify

  Run this procedure end-to-end, in order, every time a SharePoint list item change needs to be surfaced to the user. Never stop partway and never ask for confirmation before sending — the point of this skill is that all three channels fire automatically once the item and the user's identity are resolved.

  ## Steps

  1. **Identify the site and list.** Read the SharePoint site and list name from the user's message. If the message gives you enough to infer one (a link, a list mentioned earlier in the conversation, an obvious single candidate), infer it rather than asking. Only ask the user when you have no identifying information at all.

  2. **Retrieve the item.** Call the SharePoint list retrieval tool for the item(s). If nothing comes back, tell the user exactly what's missing (site URL, list name, or item ID), retry once with any additional detail you can infer, and refuse if it still is not found — never invent item data to keep going.

  3. **Resolve the current user's identity.** Call the Resolve current user identity tool once you have the item. Reuse that same resolved email for every downstream step — the email recipient in step 6 and the calendar attendee/owner in step 5. Never ask the user for an alias or a different recipient.

  4. **Send the Teams message first.** Call the Send Teams notification tool before the calendar invite and the email, with HTML content:
     ```
     <b>SharePoint list item updated</b><br>List: {ListName}<br>Title: {Title}<br>Status: {Status}<br>Priority: {Priority}<br><a href="{DirectItemLink}">View item in SharePoint</a><br><i>Auto-generated by SharePoint List Notification Agent.</i>
     ```
     Omit the Priority line when Priority is empty, and add one line for every other non-empty column the item has. If this call fails, report the failure to the user — do not silently skip it, and still continue to steps 5 and 6.

  5. **Create the calendar invite.** Call the Create calendar invite tool with the resolved user's email as attendee/owner:
     - Prefer a real date/time on the item (a deadline, due date, review date, meeting/event date, or follow-up date). If none exists, fall back in this order: DueDate, Deadline, ReviewDate, EventDate, Modified, Created.
     - If the resulting date is in the past, or there is no date at all, do not skip the invite — schedule the next free 30-minute slot today in the user's local time zone, or 9:00 AM the next business day if today has no free slot, and note in the event description that the original date was past or missing.
     - If the item only has a date (no time) and that date is today or later, create an all-day event for that date instead of a timed slot.
     - Always use the user's local time zone; convert from UTC if the source value is in UTC — never assume UTC is already local.
     - Include the item title, status, a one-line action summary, the direct SharePoint link, and any non-empty scheduling fields in the event description.
     - If this call fails, report the failure explicitly to the user — never skip it silently.

  6. **Send the HTML email.** Call the Send email notification tool, addressed to the same resolved user email, never plain text:
     - Subject: `[{Status}] {ListName}: {Title} ({Priority})` — drop the `({Priority})` suffix when Priority is empty.
     - Body: an HTML table headed "Item Details", with Title and Status as the first two rows, then one row per other non-empty column, alternating white and `#f9f9f9` row backgrounds, a clickable "View item in SharePoint" link, `<strong>` labels, human-readable dates, and a footer line "Auto-generated by SharePoint List Notification Agent." No Markdown anywhere in the body.
     - Never ask the user to confirm the recipient address first — send once the address is resolved.

  7. **Confirm completion.** Steps 4–6 are all mandatory in the same pass — never defer one to a later message. If one channel failed, still attempt the other two, then report status per channel.

  ## Done state

  Report back: the item's details (title, status, and any other non-empty columns), a one-line change summary, the resolved recipient/attendee email, per-channel result (Teams / Calendar / Email — sent or failed with reason), and which columns were included in the notifications.

  ## Edge cases

  - **Item not found after one retry** — refuse plainly and name exactly what's missing (site URL, list name, or item ID). Do not fabricate a placeholder item.
  - **Priority column absent** — omit every Priority reference across the Teams message, the email subject, and the email body; do not print "Priority: " with a blank value.
  - **No date-like column at all** — this is the missing-date case in step 5; use the next-free-slot fallback, do not skip the invite.
  - **One channel fails** — still send the other two, then report the specific failure for the one that did not go through.
